--- body ---
## This is a tracking ticket

Decomposed 2026-08-29. This body is the rationale; the executable work is in the
children below, each sized to one sign-off unit and each blocked by its real
prerequisite, never by this tracker. The Actions section further down is the
partition's source and is kept for the reasoning behind each step, not as a work
queue.

| child | scope | blocked by |
|---|---|---|
| `0261` | Enumerate multilingual ONNX candidates into a declared registry | -- |
| `0262` | The registry-driven resumable sweep harness in `bench/` | 0261 |
| `0263` | CPU arm: cost and fidelity, every candidate, every loadable dtype | 0262 |
| `0264` | GPU arm on padme; what `auto` actually selects (needs-human: no GPU here) | 0262 |
| `0265` | Recall at the deployed dtype, and the fused RRF delta | 0263 |
| `0266` | Cross-lingual probe on the multilingual vectors | 0263 |
| `0267` | The recommendation: evidence plus a decision rule | 0263 0264 0265 0266 |
| `0268` | Propose C3's replacement value and re-pin R20's gate (needs-human: his ledger) | 0267 |

Two children are labelled `needs-human` for different reasons, and neither is a
judgement call deferred out of caution. `0264` needs padme: the working machine
has no GPU at all, so nothing in that arm can be executed here beyond the code
path. `0268` needs the author's ruling: its exit criterion is a ratified entry
in `spec/DECISIONS.md`, and the ledger is his.

This tracker closes after the integration review across all eight, not on the
bare event of the last child merging.

## Context

**Every model measured in `0025-x1-recall/` is disqualified by R7.** The
requirement is not new and not soft: "the default path works for French, German,
Vietnamese, Greek and Russian with no configuration, and the default embedder is
multilingual" (`spec/REQUIREMENTS.md` R7). `all-MiniLM-L6-v2` is English.
`bge-small-en-v1.5` says so in its name. `nomic-embed-text-v1.5` is
English-centric. So is `Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B` in the form we measured. The
campaign that produced `task-recall.json`, `embed-feasibility.json`,
`gpu-feasibility.json`, `query-cost*.json` and the dtype ladders benchmarked a
field of candidates that R7 already excludes, and its recall column cannot
select anything. That is the reason for this ticket, and it should be said
plainly rather than discovered by the next reader.

The one multilingual model anywhere in this repo's evidence is Zotero core's
second model, `multilingual-e5-small` — and it has never been loaded here. It is
a blank column, not a low score.

Ticket 0220 makes dtype configurable and the device automatic. It deliberately
ships no default. This ticket produces the evidence for one, and it must
generate that evidence rather than inherit it, because of what the partial
fidelity run already shows.

**Recall measured at fp32 does not survive quantization.** nomic-768 q8 against
its own ONNX fp32, 600 passages: mean cosine 0,90031, **zero** vectors reaching
`cboulanger/zotero-rag`'s 0,999 compatibility threshold, overlap@30 of 0,7439
with a p05 of 0,5667. The fp32 rung scored against itself returns 1,0000 on
every column, so the probe discriminates and the q8 row is not an artifact of a
broken scorer. The best single neighbour survives (top-1 kept 0,9950) while a
quarter of the top-30 reshuffles. Consequence for this ticket: the 0,4639 in
`task-recall.json` is an fp32 number, and a q8 deployment does not get it. Every
recall figure here must be measured on vectors produced by the exact dtype that
would ship.

Whether that 0,90 is specific to nomic or general to 8-bit quantization is
**unknown and worth knowing**. A mean cosine of 0,90 is low for 8-bit, where
0,99+ is typical. Report the observation; do not adopt a cause. If some
candidates quantize to 0,99 and others to 0,90, quantization robustness is
itself a selection criterion, and nobody in the surveyed field measures it
(`spec/FIELD-REVIEW.md`).

## Scope

**Quantization: fp32, fp16, q8, uint8. Nothing below.** Ruled by the author, and
the measurements support the floor: on both models swept, q4 downloads larger
than q8, holds more memory and runs slower, and bnb4 is 5,1x (nomic) and 8,7x
(Qwen3) slower than q8 for lack of an optimised CPU kernel
(`dtype-ladder-nomic.json`, `dtype-ladder-qwen3-06b.json`). A rung dominated on
every cost axis cannot be rescued by quality, so measuring it wastes the run.

**Both 8-bit variants are swept, and collapsing them to one would be a mistake
made blind.** They are the same price and demonstrably not the same quality.
On nomic-768 (`quant-fidelity-nomic.json`), uint8 holds mean cosine 0,94793
against q8's 0,90031, keeps 0,8406 of the top-30 against 0,7439, and never loses
the top-1 neighbour where q8 loses it sometimes — at a download of identical
size and RAM and latency inside the ±2,3 MB / ±4,3 ms noise floor. Asymmetric
quantization preserved that model's geometry better than symmetric, for free.
Whether that ordering holds for any other model is UNTESTED, and that is exactly
why both cells are run per candidate rather than one being assumed: the point is
not that uint8 wins, it is that nobody can know which wins without measuring.

**`q8` and `int8` may resolve to the same file, so `int8` is not a third cell —
verify rather than assume.** On nomic they are one blob (md5 `b65952d0…`) and
their vectors came out bit-identical to five decimals, so measuring both
measured nothing twice over and briefly produced a spurious "int8 is the fastest
rung" from pure noise. That is a per-repository property, not a rule. The
harness must hash the resolved ONNX file per cell and, on a collision, record
the cell as a duplicate of the one already measured instead of spending a run on
it — which also stops the noise from ever being read as a difference again.

Note the interaction with hardware, which is why fp16 is in scope despite
failing here: **fp16 does not load on the ONNX CPU provider** — nomic aborts at
session init with `Attempting to get index by a name which does not exist:
InsertedPrecisionFreeCast_/emb_ln/Constant_output_0 for node
/emb_ln/Mul/SimplifiedLayerNormFusion`, a graph-fusion failure rather than a
missing kernel. fp16 is therefore a GPU-only rung, and the CPU and GPU arms of
this study do not have the same shape. Do not silently drop it on CPU; record it
as unloadable, which is a result.

**Models: multilingual only.** An English-only model is rejected on R7 without
being measured — that is the filter, applied before the benchmark rather than as
a tiebreak after it. Candidates must also publish ONNX under the file names
transformers.js resolves, which is a sharper constraint than it looks (below).

**Memory is a reported cost, not a gate.** Ruled 2026-08-29
(`spec/DECISIONS.md`): R7 is hard and C3's 300 MB ceiling gives way where the
two conflict. That inverts what an RSS number does in this study. It does not
reject a candidate — applied as a gate it would reject every multilingual model
measured — it is reported, and the winner's figure becomes the input to C3's
replacement value, which is ratified after this ticket and not before. So
measure RSS carefully and rank on quality, not the reverse.

Three cells are already measured, warm, five fresh processes each, q8, and they
are the baseline this study extends rather than repeats:

| model | RSS median | spread | note |
|---|---|---|---|
| `nomic-embed-text-v1.5` | 234,2 MB | 6,6 | English, R7-ineligible, kept as the contrast |
| `granite-embedding-97m-multilingual-r2` | 407,8 MB | 2,5 | cheapest multilingual measured |
| `multilingual-e5-small` | 419,5 MB | 6,9 | Zotero core's pick |

The two multilingual figures are 11,7 MB apart against a spread of up to 6,9, so
they are close and not ranked. Do not report an ordering between them without
more runs than this.

**This ticket will break the RSS gate, and that is expected.** R20's gate
(`check-slow`, DESIGN §2.8) asserts `server p95 <= 300 MB` verbatim, against the
budget C3 ratified. The 2026-08-29 ruling makes that number wrong for a
multilingual default, and every candidate here measures 405 MB or more — so the
first time one is resident the gate goes red for the right reason. Do NOT
loosen or disable it while the study runs: a gate turned off to make a
measurement pass is how ticket 0009's defect happened. Leave it red, and re-pin
it in the SAME change that sets C3's replacement value, once the chosen
embedder's figure exists. Until then the red gate is the reminder that C3 and
the documents disagree.

**Do not carry the vocabulary explanation forward.** An earlier reading of these
numbers held that the embedding table sets the resident floor and quantization
cannot shrink it, with a `vocab x dim` predictor to match. It was retracted:
Granite and e5-small differ by 102,6 MB of fp32 embedding table and by 11,7 MB
of resident memory, and the predictor missed Granite by 106 MB on its first
out-of-sample test after matching e5-small to a tenth of a megabyte because it
had been fitted on e5-small. File sizes still follow the table; resident sizes
are unexplained. If this study wants a memory model, it must earn one — and it
does not need one to pick a model.

**Hardware: CPU here, GPU on padme.** Both arms, same corpus, same probes, same
driver. `gpu-feasibility.json`'s A4000 figures were derived from PyTorch run
logs on another machine and are upper bounds by projection, not measurements in
the shipped runtime; this ticket replaces them with real ONNX numbers on the
designated GPU host. NOTE: the working machine (`doudou`) has no GPU at all —
`nvidia-smi` is absent — so nothing in the GPU arm can be developed and tested
here beyond the code path.

## Candidate field, enumerated 2026-08-29

A search over open, small, genuinely multilingual embedders with ONNX under the
filenames transformers.js resolves. Seed for the registry; not a shortlist, and
not verified beyond what each column says.

| model | repo to load | params | dim | max seq | licence | claimed score |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| granite-embedding-97m-multilingual-r2 | `onnx-community/granite-embedding-97m-multilingual-r2-ONNX` | 97,4M | 384 | 32 768 | Apache-2.0 (both repos) | 60,3 MTEB ML Retrieval (18) |
| granite-embedding-311m-multilingual-r2 | `onnx-community/granite-embedding-311m-multilingual-r2-ONNX` | 311,7M | 768 MRL | measured: see registry | Apache-2.0 (both) | 65,2 same benchmark |
| snowflake-arctic-embed-m-v2.0 | `Snowflake/snowflake-arctic-embed-m-v2.0` (no mirror needed) | 305,4M | 768 MRL→256 | 8 192 | Apache-2.0 | MIRACL(4) nDCG@10 55,2 |
| gte-multilingual-base | `onnx-community/gte-multilingual-base` | 305,4M | 768 MRL 128–768 | 8 192 | Apache-2.0 | charts only, no plain figure |
| multilingual-e5-base | `Xenova/multilingual-e5-base` | 278,0M | 768 | measured: see registry | MIT | none aggregate |
| multilingual-e5-small | `Xenova/multilingual-e5-small` | 117,7M | 384 | 512 | MIT | 50,9, per IBM's card |
| paraphrase-multilingual-mpnet-base-v2 | `Xenova/paraphrase-multilingual-mpnet-base-v2` | 278,0M | 768 | 128 | Apache-2.0 | none |
| paraphrase-multilingual-MiniLM-L12-v2 | `Xenova/paraphrase-multilingual-MiniLM-L12-v2` | 117,7M | 384 | 128 | Apache-2.0 | none |
| distiluse-base-multilingual-cased-v2 | `Xenova/distiluse-base-multilingual-cased-v2` | 134,7M | 512 | 128 | Apache-2.0 | none |

The 128-token ceiling on the three sentence-transformers entries is a hard
disqualifier for passage retrieval at this project's chunk geometry; keep them
in the registry as measured negatives rather than deleting them.

Rejected with reasons: `google/embeddinggemma-300m` on licence (Gemma terms,
not open) despite a clean technical fit; `jina-embeddings-v3` on licence
(CC-BY-NC) and size; `bge-m3` (568M) and `snowflake-arctic-embed-l-v2.0`
(567,8M) on size; both Granite **v1** variants because their declared language
list is twelve and omits Greek, Vietnamese and Russian entirely — a reminder to
read the language list rather than the word "multilingual".
`jina-embeddings-v2-base-multilingual` is a **could-not-look**: gated, 401 to an
unauthenticated fetch. `nomic-embed-text-v2-moe` is a confirmed absence — no
ONNX anywhere, checked through the search endpoint rather than inferred from a
401.

**The claimed scores are vendor numbers and two of them come from the same
card.** IBM's README reports both its own 60,3 and e5-small's 50,9. Use them to
choose what to measure; never to choose what to ship.

**The `max seq` column is superseded by the registry.** Child `0261` measured
these from each repository's own `config.json`, and two cells of this seed table
were wrong: the 311m mirror declares a quarter of what its upstream repo does,
and e5-base declares 514 rather than 512. Both cells above now point at
`bench/models.json`, which owns the measured value and records where it was read.
The remaining columns of this table stay as filed — a seed, not a measurement.

## The ONNX-naming constraint, which prunes the candidate list first

`dtype` resolves through `DEFAULT_DTYPE_SUFFIX_MAPPING` to a specific filename,
so a model is only sweepable if its repo publishes those names. Verified
2026-08-29:

- `intfloat/multilingual-e5-small` publishes `model.onnx`, `model_O4.onnx`,
  `model_qint8_avx512_vnni.onnx` — **the transformers.js dtype knob cannot
  address any of these but fp32.** The original author's repo is not usable for
  a dtype sweep.
- `Xenova/multilingual-e5-small` publishes the full conventional set
  (`model_fp16`, `model_quantized`, `model_int8`, `model_uint8`, `model_q4`,
  `model_q4f16`, `model_bnb4`), and so does
  `Xenova/paraphrase-multilingual-MiniLM-L12-v2`.
- `Alibaba-NLP/gte-multilingual-base` and `nomic-ai/nomic-embed-text-v2-moe`
  publish **no ONNX at all**, so neither is a candidate today whatever its
  quality.
- `onnx-community/*` mirrors returned HTTP 401 to an unauthenticated query in
  this session; that is a "could not look", not an absence. Re-probe before
  concluding a mirror does not exist.

So step one is enumeration, not benchmarking, and its output is a list of
`(model, repo, available dtypes)` with the unavailable combinations recorded as
unavailable rather than omitted. A candidate that only exists at fp32 is a
finding about that candidate.

## Infrastructure: the deliverable is a harness, not a table

The explicit requirement is that adding a model later costs one line and a run,
not a rewrite. Concretely:

- **A declared candidate registry** — one file listing `(id, hf repo, dim,
  languages, licence, MRL yes/no, notes)`. Adding a model means adding a record.
  Nothing else in the harness names a model.
- **Results keyed by `(model, dtype, device, corpus, driver version)`**, one
  file per cell, written incrementally. A partial sweep is a valid state: the
  scorer reads what exists and reports what is missing rather than failing. This
  matters because the runs are long and will be interrupted.
- **Idempotent and resumable** — a completed cell is skipped, so re-running
  after adding a candidate measures only the new cells. The existing
  `sweep.sh`/`fidsweep.sh` pattern (skip when the output exists) is the seed;
  promote it into the repo instead of leaving it in a scratch directory.
- **Reuse the existing drivers**: `bench/query_embed_cost.mjs` (RAM, latency,
  batch 1), `bench/quant_fidelity.mjs` (vectors at a rung),
  `bench/quant_fidelity_score.py` (fidelity and rank agreement),
  `bench/merge_dtype_ladder.py` (cost ladder), `bench/vec_task_recall.mjs`
  (recall). All four already take `--dtype`/`--device` or are dtype-agnostic.
  Do not write a fifth thing that overlaps them.
- **One process per cell**, as the ladder sweeps already do, so RSS is what a
  fresh server pays rather than a delta against a warmed allocator. The existing
  MiniLM and bge figures were taken sequentially in one process and are
  therefore NOT comparable to nomic's; re-measure them rather than reusing them.
- **Warm only, and never mix a cold run into a table.** A cold run pays the
  weight download inside the measured window. Granite-97m read 364,8 MB at q8
  cold against 410,2 warm, and 453,5 at uint8 cold against 404,9 warm — 45 to
  49 MB of error, in both directions, which is larger than the differences this
  study exists to resolve. The harness downloads first, then measures.
- **Repeat every RSS cell and report the median with its spread.** Warm, over
  five fresh processes, the spread is 2,5 to 6,9 MB. So a warm figure is good to
  about 10 MB and no better, and any ranking that turns on less than that is
  reading noise. Granite at 407,8 and e5-small at 419,5 are close, not ranked.
- **A per-model input template in the registry.** e5 models require `query: `
  and `passage: ` prefixes — its card says omitting them costs performance — and
  other families need none. A harness that ignores this under-measures e5 and
  would conclude an English model retrieves better. The template belongs beside
  the model id, applied by the driver, and recorded in every result file.

## Engine tracking: ONNX versions, limits, and where it is going

A measurement here is only valid for the engine that produced it, and this
engine moves. Record with every result, and investigate as its own deliverable:

- **Versions**: `@huggingface/transformers` (4.2.0 here), `onnxruntime-node`
  (1.24.3), `onnxruntime-web` (1.26.0-dev.20260416-b7804b056c),
  `onnxruntime-common` (1.24.3). These are not one version; the web build is a
  dev snapshot ahead of the node build, and a result can depend on which is
  used.
- **The engine documents its own support matrix in source.**
  `src/backends/onnx.js` carries a table "updated as of ONNX Runtime
  1.23.0-dev.20250612-70f14d7670": CUDA on Linux x64 only and CUDA v12 only, no
  CUDA on Windows or macOS or arm64; DirectML on Windows only; CoreML on macOS
  only; WebGPU everywhere but arm64 Linux and marked experimental. That table is
  the honest statement of what `device: 'auto'` can reach, and it is versioned —
  re-read it rather than citing this ticket.
- **Known limits to characterise**: fp16 on the CPU provider (above); whether
  `q4f16` fails for the same reason or a different one; whether the WebGPU path
  is usable from Node at all; what `auto` actually selects on padme, observed
  rather than inferred.
- **Development plan**: upstream `huggingface/transformers.js#945` ("use cuda by
  default and bump onnxruntime-node") was **open** at last check, its author
  reporting ~10x and stating he could not run the tests. If it lands, the
  meaning of the default changes and every CPU baseline here needs re-reading.
  Track it and record its state with the results.

## Actions

1. Enumerate multilingual candidates with usable ONNX. Output the
   `(model, repo, dtypes available)` registry described above, with 401s and
   absences distinguished. Include `Xenova/multilingual-e5-small` (Zotero core's
   own multilingual pick, the blank column) and
   `Xenova/paraphrase-multilingual-MiniLM-L12-v2`; probe `bge-m3`,
   `jina-embeddings-v3`, `snowflake-arctic-embed` and the `onnx-community`
   mirrors with authentication before ruling them out.
2. Build the registry-driven, resumable harness; promote the scratch sweep
   scripts into `bench/`.
3. Run the CPU arm per candidate: fp32, fp16 (expected unloadable — record it),
   q8 and uint8. Cost first, since it is cheap and prunes. Hash each resolved
   ONNX file and skip a cell whose file was already measured, recording it as a
   duplicate.
4. Run the GPU arm on padme, same cells. Record what `auto` selects.
5. Measure recall **at each surviving dtype**, on vectors that dtype produced —
   not fidelity-to-fp32 as a proxy for it. Fidelity stays as a second, cheaper
   signal and as the quantization-robustness criterion.
6. Measure the **fused** delta, not the vector-arm delta: RRF over FTS5 plus
   vectors is what a user sees, and a vector-arm gain can wash out against an
   unchanged keyword arm. This has never been measured
   (`0070-cosine-fusion/` is loop fusion, a different thing).
7. Add a cross-lingual probe. R7 is per-language lanes; ticket 0037 is the
   stronger property (an EN or FR query finding Vietnamese content). A
   multilingual embedder is the mechanism for both, so measure it here while the
   vectors exist rather than re-running later. Coordinate with 0037; do not
   duplicate its requirement text.
8. Write the recommendation as evidence plus a decision rule, addressed to the
   maintainer's choice, not as a patch to a default.

## Test

The harness gets tests before the campaign, because a benchmark that silently
measures the wrong thing produces confident wrong answers for weeks:

- The registry is the only place a model name appears — an adherence-style test
  greps `bench/` for hard-coded model ids outside it.
- A cell already present is skipped and NOT recomputed; a cell absent is run.
  Assert both directions, since "skips everything" and "resumes correctly" look
  identical on a full directory.
- The scorer reports a missing cell as missing and still scores the rest, rather
  than failing or silently reporting a short table.
- Positive control on the fidelity scorer: fp32 against itself must return
  cosine 1,0 and overlap 1,0. This is already how the nomic q8 result was known
  to be real rather than a broken probe; make it an assertion.
- A recorded engine-version mismatch between a result file and the running
  environment is surfaced, not ignored.

## Verification

- [ ] Every English-only model is absent from the results, by the R7 filter, and
      the registry records why rather than leaving a gap
- [ ] Each result file names its four engine versions and the device actually
      selected, not the device requested
- [ ] `auto` on padme is observed to select a GPU provider — the claim that it
      does is currently read from source and has never been executed
- [ ] Adding one candidate to the registry and re-running measures only the new
      cells; demonstrate it by adding one
- [ ] Recall figures come from vectors at the deployed dtype; no fp32 recall is
      quoted for a quantized configuration anywhere in the report
- [ ] The fp32-against-itself control returns 1,0 in the shipped scorer

## Invariants

- R7 is a filter, not a tiebreak: no English-only model is benchmarked
- R10 holds throughout: no API embedder enters the comparison, on any arm
- No second embedding set is proposed — the field survey found 0 of ~20 projects
  maintaining one, and the single coexistence implementation serves migration
- Existing artifacts in `0025-x1-recall/` are not deleted or rewritten. They are
  a valid record of an invalid candidate field, and the honest move is to say so
  where they are cited
- The recommendation does not set a default; it equips the maintainer to set
  one, and states what evidence would overturn it

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Registry-driven resumable harness in `bench/`, tested as above
- [ ] CPU and GPU arms complete for every surviving candidate at fp32, fp16, q8
      and uint8, with unloadable combinations recorded as results and duplicate
      files recorded as duplicates rather than re-measured
- [ ] The q8-versus-uint8 ordering is reported per candidate, so the question of
      whether nomic's result generalises is answered rather than assumed
- [ ] Recall at deployed dtype, fidelity, and the fused delta measured
- [ ] Engine versions, limits and the state of the upstream CUDA work recorded
      with the results
- [ ] A recommendation stating a decision rule, its evidence, and what would
      overturn it
- [ ] C3's replacement value proposed from the chosen embedder's measured RSS, with
      R20's gate re-pinned in the same change — never before it, and never by
      loosening the gate to make a measurement pass
